<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes van Walbeeck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1602">1602</a>–c. 1649</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes van Walbeeck was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> navigator and commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>. In 1634 he led the expedition that captured Curaçao from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, giving the company a naval base, salt pans and a foothold close to the Spanish Main. He became the island's first Dutch governor, and Aruba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonaire">Bonaire</a> were taken soon afterwards.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Van Walbeeck, also called Jan or Johan, was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, perhaps the Jan van Walbeeck, son of the merchant Jacob van Walbeeck and Weijntgen van Foreest, who was baptised there on 15 August 1602. He studied at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> and then sailed as navigator and cartographer on De Amsterdam in the Nassau fleet of Admiral Jacques l'Hermite, which circled the world from 1623 to 1626. The account of the voyage that Hessel Gerritsz published in 1626 is thought to be his work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After further study at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>, a diplomatic mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> with Laurens Reael and a voyage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, he left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> (WIC) in 1629. In April 1630 he reached Pernambuco on the Neptunus, soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forces had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> from the Portuguese. He joined the Politieke Raad, the highest government body of Dutch Brazil, and became its president in 1632. After a Spanish fleet destroyed the WIC settlement on Sint Maarten in 1633, the company's council planned a base on Curaçao for its salt pans and harbour.</p><p>Van Walbeeck sailed on 4 May 1634 with four ships carrying 180 sailors and 250 soldiers under the Huguenot mercenary Pierre Le Grand. Currents and winds kept them out of St Anna Bay until 29 July, when they entered and took the island without loss of life. The 32 Spaniards and fewer than 500 Arawaks withdrew west after poisoning their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, and on 21 August the Spanish commander, Lope Lopez de Morla, surrendered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> deported most of them to Coro, keeping about seventy-five Arawaks as labourers, and built Fort Amsterdam, finished in 1635, beside which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad</a> began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1638 Jacob Pietersz Tolck succeeded him as governor, and van Walbeeck returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, sitting on the Hoge Raad until 1642. He left Brazil in 1647 and was living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> when his wife was buried there on 29 April 1649. Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a> he was one of the few WIC servants with a university education, and it has been suggested that he missed the post of director-general of New Netherland only by being away at the wrong time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johannes van Walbeeck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dutch West India Company captured Curaçao from Spain in 1634 under <b>Johannes van Walbeeck</b>, seeking a naval base, salt and a foothold near the Spanish Main.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-curacao">Colony of Curaçao</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.37,4.88&amp;year=1632" aria-label="Amsterdam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johannes van Walbeeck"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 5641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 5641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.37,4.88&amp;year=1632">What was here in 1632? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
